/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A pure fluid has one chemical species.
   - Plot all such states with P up, V to the right and T going back.
   - Cut the surface three ways.
   - Each view answers a different question.
   - For water at 1 bar the two ends of the two-phase region are vf = 0.001043 m³/kg (saturated liquid) and vg = 1.694 m³/kg (saturated vapour).
   - A liquid far from the critical point has nearly vertical P–V isotherms, so its volume hardly changes.
   - Below the critical temperature a P–V isotherm has three parts.
   - The flat part is the two-phase region.
   - Repeat for every temperature below the critical temperature Tc.
   - The two curves meet at the critical point: for water, Tc = 374 °C and Pc = 221 bar.
*/
