/* book: lead: The ideal-gas law, PV = nRT, is the simplest equation of state. It treats molecules as points with no attractions, so heat, work and internal-energy change follow from the path alone. Engineers use it for a first estimate of compressor duty, pipeline pressure drop or the air in a tyre, and it is the reference against which real gases are measured. This chapter fixes the sign convention and works t */
/* book Figure 2.2.1: Worked example 1 on a P–V plot: 1 mol at 300 K is squeezed from 24.94 L to 12.47 L. The isothermal path ends at 2.00 bar, the adiabatic path at 2.64 bar because compression heats the gas to 396 K, and the isobaric path stays at 1.00 bar because the gas is cooled to 150 K. */
