/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The ideal-gas law is PV = nRT, with P the absolute pressure, V the volume, n the moles and T the absolute temperature.
   - In this model molecules have no volume and no forces except elastic collisions, so U depends on T only, and so does H = U + PV = U + nRT.
   - P is absolute pressure; V is volume; n is moles; R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ = 0.08314 L bar mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; T is absolute temperature in K; Cp and Cv are molar heat capacities at constant pressure and volume; γ is the heat-capacity ratio.
   - Here Cv = (∂U/∂T)V is the heat to raise 1 mol by 1 K at constant volume and Cp = (∂H/∂T)P the same at constant pressure.
   - Use W as work done by the gas on the surroundings.
   - ΔU is change in internal energy; Q is heat added to the gas; W is work done by the gas on the surroundings.
   - Each path fixes one quantity.
   - An isochoric path has no volume change, so W = 0; all heat goes into U.
   - Some syllabi call these non-flow processes.
   - For a mixture of ideal gases each component obeys PiV = niRT, with Pi its partial pressure, and P = ΣPi (Dalton's law).
*/
