/* book Worked example 2: Q: For the same three paths of worked example 1, take Cv = (5/2)R = 20.785 J/(mol·K) and Cp = (7/2)R = 29.099 J/(mol·K). Find W (by the gas), Q and ΔU for each path, and check ΔU = Q − W.
   1. (a) Isothermal at 300 K: W = nRT ln(V2/V1) = 1 × 8.314 × 300 × ln 0.5 = −1728.8 J. ΔU = 0, so Q = W = −1728.8 J: the work put in leaves as heat.
   2. (b) Adiabatic, T2 = 395.85 K: Q = 0. W = nCv(T1 − T2) = 20.785 × (300 − 395.85) = −1992.3 J. ΔU = Q − W = +1992.3 J, all stored in the gas.
   3. (c) Isobaric at 1.00 bar, T2 = 150 K: W = PΔV = 100 000 Pa × (12.47 − 24.94) × 10−3 m³ = −1247.0 J.
   4. Heat for (c): Q = nCpΔT = 29.099 × (150 − 300) = −4364.9 J. Energy change: ΔU = nCvΔT = 20.785 × (−150) = −3117.8 J.
   5. Check (c): Q − W = −4364.9 − (−1247.0) = −3117.9 J, equal to ΔU within rounding. Negative W everywhere means work is done on the gas, as expected for a compression.
   Answer:  */
