/* book exam traps: PVγ = constant is for adiabatic, not isothermal. Isothermal is PV = constant; using γ there gives a wrong final pressure. | Put T in kelvin. 27 °C is 300 K; using 27 in PV = nRT is a large error. | State the W sign convention. Many engineering texts take W by gas positive (ΔU = Q − W); many chemistry texts take W on gas positive (ΔU = Q + W). Mixing them flips Q. | For a rigid tank use Cv, not Cp. Q = nCvΔT for isochoric; Q = nCpΔT for isobaric. | Adiabatic does not mean isothermal. Q = 0, but T changes and W = nCv(T1 − T2) is not zero. */
